# D4.5.12 — Observation-Family Minimality & Quotient Realizability

The attached material reinforces the separation:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Eliminability}
\neq
\text{Preservation}
\neq
\text{Realization}
}
$$

and, independently,

$$
\boxed{
\text{Requirement-faithfulness}
\neq
\text{Minimality}.
}
$$

That distinction is crucial. The current D4.5 theorem proves that

$$
\ker_{\rm rep}(\rho_{\mathcal O})
\subseteq
\sim_{\rm req}^{Q,\Gamma}
$$

is exactly the right criterion for a requirement-faithful observation representation. But it does **not** tell us whether \(\mathcal O\) contains unnecessary observables.

## The next research question

We should therefore formulate:

> **Given \(Q,\Gamma\) and the required-distinction relation \(\sim_{\rm req}^{Q,\Gamma}\), when does an admissible observation family generate exactly the required quotient, and when is that family minimal?**

There are three progressively stronger questions.

### R1 — Faithfulness

Does

$$
\boxed{
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
\subseteq
\sim_{\rm req}^{Q,\Gamma}
}
$$

hold?

If yes, \(\mathcal O\) preserves all required distinctions.

### R2 — Exact realizability

Does there exist an admissible \(\mathcal O\) such that

$$
\boxed{
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
=
\sim_{\rm req}^{Q,\Gamma}?
}
$$

This is stronger.

It asks whether the required-distinction quotient can actually be **realized by observations**.

### R3 — Minimality

If such families exist, which one is minimal?

For inclusion minimality:

$$
\boxed{
\mathcal O^\ast
\text{ is minimal}
\iff
\equiv_{\mathcal O^\ast}
\subseteq
\sim_{\rm req}
}
$$

and

$$
\forall\mathcal O'\subsetneq\mathcal O^\ast:
\quad
\equiv_{\mathcal O'}
\nsubseteq
\sim_{\rm req}.
$$

For cardinality minimality:

$$
\boxed{
|\mathcal O^\ast|
=
\min_{\mathcal O\in\mathfrak O_{\rm adm}}
|\mathcal O|
}
$$

subject to

$$
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
\subseteq
\sim_{\rm req}.
$$

