# One further mathematical distinction we should add

There is a potentially even more fundamental issue.

An observation family can be requirement-faithful **without being exact**:

$$
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
\subsetneq
\sim_{\rm req}.
$$

That means the observations distinguish **more states than the inquiry requires**.

Conversely, if

$$
\equiv_{\mathcal O}
\supsetneq
\sim_{\rm req},
$$

then some required distinctions have been collapsed.

So we obtain three regimes:

$$
\boxed{
\begin{array}{rcl}
\equiv_{\mathcal O}\subsetneq\sim_{\rm req}
&:&
\text{over-resolving representation}\\[3pt]
\equiv_{\mathcal O}=\sim_{\rm req}
&:&
\text{exact requirement representation}\\[3pt]
\equiv_{\mathcal O}\not\subseteq\sim_{\rm req}
&:&
\text{requirement-inadequate representation}.
\end{array}
}
$$

This is valuable because it gives us a precise meaning of **representation excess** without yet introducing entropy, information metrics, topology, probability, or complexity.

